## 7. Risks & Mitigations

| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Single maintainer; live-platform incidents preempt roadmap work | Timeline slip | Explicit capacity allocation (D4); ranges already assume [X%]; maintenance backlog triaged before Phase 1 start |
| Q4 answered "ongoing" late in Phase 1 | Rework; timeline swing | D2 required *before* Phase 1 estimate is committed |
| Cardinality assumptions run deeper than audit reveals | Phase 1 scope growth | Audit is workstream #1, before build; static analysis tooling reduces discovery risk |
| Validation source of truth is external and shifting | Validation rework | Rules live in the reference data layer from day one (Phase 1 WS4); updates are data changes, not code changes |
| Premature abstraction pressure ("build the platform now") | Phase 2 built on speculation | Sequencing argument in §3; phases gated on evidence |
| Pressure to date the Integration track | Speculative commitments on unscoped vendor APIs | Gates-only policy stated in §4.4; per-vendor sizing at gate opening; D7 de-risks the longest lead item early |
| VMS vendor API variability / immaturity | C3 cost blowout on a given vendor | Per-vendor scoping before any C3 commitment; priority order (D6) puts the most tractable vendor first |
| Reference layer scope creep (tables added speculatively) | Catalog becomes its own project | Growth rule: one table per evidenced defect; ingestion boundary is the only Phase 2 commitment |
| Pilot client unavailability | Phase 1 exit blocked | Fallback: internal dry-run with implementation team as proxy respondent (reduced confidence, noted at exit) |

---

## 8. Open Questions

| # | Question | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | Which master data domains, and how many, for the pilot? | Sets Phase 1 template scope |
| Q2 | One client contact or several (per-domain owners)? | Multiple owners ≈ multi-respondent within one org; changes portal/provisioning design |
| Q3 | Who defines "valid" for master data — our rules, or VMS configuration requirements? | Determines the sourcing for capability C1 and the initial reference tables |
| Q4 | **One-time (implementation) collection, or ongoing maintenance?** | **Largest timeline swing in the plan: 6–8 weeks vs. 10–14 weeks for Phase 1** |
| Q5 | Is a pilot implementation available, and what is its go-live date? | Converts the plan into a schedule; provides the Phase 1 forcing function |
| Q6 | Which VMS platforms, and in what priority order? | Sequences Track C scoping (D6); vendors differ materially in API maturity |
| Q7 | Do we have — or can we obtain — API and sandbox access for the priority VMS platforms? | C3's gate; procurement lead time is why D7 initiates at Phase 1 exit |
| Q8 | *(Dormant — only if OPT-1 triggers)* Do target client tenants support external guest access and the required SharePoint licensing? | Feasibility precondition for the hosted-channel spike |

